window.PHASE_DATA_LN=window.PHASE_DATA_LN||{};window.PHASE_DATA_LN[6]={"pages":[{"pdf":141,"printed":126,"ch":6,"text":"CHAPTER 6\n\nLayer 5: Asset Pricing, Event Studies\nand Survey Evidence\nBefore you start: concepts needed (stochastic discount factor, returns and risk premia, abnormal returns, survey methods), a map of the readings, and suggested reading order This layer has three very different readings: a textbook (Cochrane), a methods paper (MacKinlay) and an opinion survey (Stroebel and Wurgler). They answer one question from three directions: how would you know whether climate risk is priced, and what do experts think? You can follow these notes without having read the originals. The notes below assume only the ideas on this page.\n\nConcepts you need first\n• Return and excess return. A return is (end price + payoff) / start price. An excess return subtracts the risk-free rate. Everything in asset pricing is about why excess returns differ across assets.\n• Risk premium, beta, alpha. A risk premium is the average excess return an investor demands for bearing a risk. A beta measures how much an asset moves with a risk factor (a regression slope). An alpha is the average return left over after the factor explains what it can; a good model has alphas near zero.\n• Covariance and the idea that only some risk is paid. An asset that does badly exactly when you are poor (a recession, a crisis) is unattractive and must offer a higher average return. Risk that you can diversify away earns nothing.\n• Stochastic discount factor (SDF), m. One number per state of the world that says how much a dollar is worth in that state. Price = expected (m × payoff). High m means ”bad times, a dollar is precious”. You do not need the utility theory behind it, only this reading of m.\n• Regression basics. What an OLS slope, a residual, an R², a t-statistic and a standard error are, and why errors that are correlated across firms make standard errors too small.\n• Abnormal return. The return on a stock beyond what it would have earned without a specific event. MacKinlay's whole method rests on this.\n• Survey vocabulary. Response rate (completed / invited), selection bias (people who answer differ from people who do not), ranking vs choose-up-to-three questions, subgroup breakdowns.\n\nMap of the readings"},{"pdf":142,"printed":127,"ch":6,"text":"Table 6.1. Before you start: Map of the readings\nReading\n\nWhat it is\n\nThe question it answers\n\nCochrane, Asset\nPricing (2005), selected\nchapters\n\nGraduate textbook\non one idea: price\n= expected\ndiscounted payoff\n\nWhy do assets earn different Theory and test design\nreturns, and how do you test for any climate factor;\na pricing model?\nstandard errors\n\nMacKinlay (1997),\nMethods survey,\nEvent Studies in\nJEL\nEconomics and Finance\nStroebel and Wurgler\n(2021), What do you\nthink about climate\nfinance?\n\nHow do you measure the\nprice effect of a specific\nannouncement, and how\nreliable is the test?\n\nExpert opinion\nWhat do finance experts\nsurvey; working\nbelieve about climate risk\npaper and appendix and whether it is priced?\n\nWhere it feeds\n\nThe method for\nmeasuring stock\nreactions to climate\nevents or rules\nMotivation and\nliterature gap;\ndiscount-rate\nbenchmark\n\nSuggested order\n1. Section 1 (Cochrane), in the order of its own sub-sections. 1.1 and 1.2 first: they hold the one idea. 1.5 next for factor models, then 1.6 for tests, 1.7 for the evidence.\n2. Section 2 (MacKinlay). Start with 2.2 (the procedure), then 2.3–2.4 (models and tests), then jump to 2.6 (clustering) and 2.8 (power), then 2.13 for your thesis.\n3. Section 3 (Stroebel and Wurgler). Read the tables in 3.3, then 3.6.\n4. Section 4. The quotables list and the thesis links. If you only have ten minutes, read\n4.1 and 4.2.\n\nWhat this layer connects to\nIt comes after Layer 4 (contagion and systemic risk) because those papers describe how shocks spread; this layer describes how markets price and test for such shocks. It is a prerequisite for Layer 6 (climate finance and the central-bank view), whose papers use the vocabulary introduced here: priced risk, factor, premium, event-based evidence.\n\n1. Cochrane, Asset Pricing: reading the file and writing the notes\nWhat was read. The file is the 2005 revised edition (553 printed pages). It is a textbook, so I read the parts your thesis can use: Chapter 1 (the core idea, pp. 3–24), Chapter 9 (factor pricing models, pp. 149–174), Chapter 12 (regression tests, pp. 229–249, up to the Fama– MacBeth discussion) and the closing summary of Chapter 20 (pp. 449–453). I did not read the rest of the book (GMM theory in Chapters 10–11, option pricing, bonds, the long-horizon material in 20.1–20.2, the equity-premium chapter 21 beyond its opening). Page numbers below are the printed book pages, not PDF pages.\n\n1.1 The one idea: price equals expected discounted payoff\nEverything in the book is one equation. An asset with payoff xₜ₊₁ has price"},{"pdf":143,"printed":128,"ch":6,"text":"pₜ = Eₜ(mₜ₊₁ xₜ₊₁) (the central formula, p. 6)\nwhere m is the stochastic discount factor (SDF). For a consumer with utility u(c) and impatience β, mₜ₊₁ = β u′(cₜ₊₁)/u′(cₜ): the marginal rate of substitution between today and tomorrow (p. 6). Intuition: a payoff is worth more if it arrives in a state where you are hungry (consumption is low, marginal utility high). Quotable (p. 7): ”All asset pricing models amount to alternative ways of connecting the stochastic discount factor to data.”\n\nThis is the sentence to use when you need to say that CAPM, ICAPM, APT, consumption models and your own climate factor are all siblings. Returns form (p. 8): a gross return R = x/p, so 1 = E(mR). Risk-free rate (pp. 10–11): Rᶠ = 1/E(m). So E(m) is the price of a sure dollar; when interest rates are high, m is small on average.\n\n1.2 Where risk premia come from\nUsing cov(m, x) = E(mx) − E(m)E(x):\n• Price with a risk correction (p. 13): p = E(x)/Rᶠ + cov(m, x). The first term is the present value for a risk-neutral investor; the covariance term is the risk adjustment. A payoff that is high when m is high (bad times) is worth more than its expected value.\n• Excess return (p. 14): E(Rⁱ) − Rᶠ = −Rᶠ cov(m, Rⁱ). Assets that pay off badly when m is high (in bad times) must offer a higher average return. This is the formal reason a bank stock or a carbon-heavy firm that collapses in a bad state must earn a premium.\n• Idiosyncratic risk gets no premium (p. 15): only the part of a payoff that covaries with m is priced. Diversifiable risk is free to ignore. Link to Layer 4: contagion and network losses matter for asset prices only to the extent they load on m.\n• Beta representation (p. 16): E(Rⁱ) = Rᶠ + βⁱ,ₘ λₘ, where β is the regression coefficient of the return on m and λ is the price of risk. With consumption as the factor, λ is proportional to risk aversion times the variance of consumption growth (p. 17).\n\n1.3 Bounds and the equity-premium puzzle\nBecause correlation cannot exceed one, the SDF must be volatile enough to explain observed Sharpe ratios (the Hansen–Jagannathan-type bound, eq. 1.17, pp. 17–19): |E(Rⁱ) − Rᶠ| / σ(Rⁱ) ≤ σ(m) / E(m). The left side is the Sharpe ratio of any asset; the right side is the volatility of the SDF. Cochrane's illustration (pp. 20–21) is that with observed US stock-market Sharpe ratios the required risk aversion in a consumption model is around 50, and about 250 if the consumptionreturn correlation is only 0.2. That is the equity premium puzzle: plausible risk aversion is far smaller. Why this matters for you: the same logic is used when someone argues that climate risk must be priced (or is not). If investors fear a climate state of the world, m must be high there, and assets that do badly there should earn a premium. The Sharpe-ratio bound also tells you how large any climate premium could be before it becomes implausible."},{"pdf":144,"printed":129,"ch":6,"text":"1.4 Predictability and the random-walk idea\npp. 22–24: if returns were unpredictable, prices would follow a random walk, but that is not a requirement of asset pricing. Expected returns can vary over time (risk aversion or risk varies), so some predictability is perfectly compatible with rational pricing. Present-value logic (p. 24) connects price to future cash flows: price moves because expected cash flows or discount rates move.\n\n1.5 Factor models: how theory gets to data (Chapter 9)\nStart (pp. 149–151): marginal utility is not observed, so approximate m by a linear function of observable factors: m = a + b′f. This is exactly equivalent to a beta-pricing model E(Rⁱ) = γ + βⁱ′λ (λ are factor risk premia). Quotable (p. 151): ”all factor models are derived as specializations of the consumption-based model.”\n\nHow each classic model picks the factor:\n• CAPM (pp. 152–157). The factor is the market return. Cochrane shows four routes to it: quadratic utility, exponential utility with normal returns, a quadratic value function, and log utility (the last uses the market portfolio as the claim on all consumption). Result: E(Rⁱ) − Rᶠ = βⁱ,ₘ [E(Rᵐ) − Rᶠ]. Only market beta is priced.\n• ICAPM (pp. 165–167). Investors also care about state variables that shift the investment opportunity set (future returns, labour income, recession risk). Extra factors are priced if they hedge those shifts.\n• APT (pp. 173–174). If returns have a factor structure with small residual risk, noarbitrage alone pins down an approximate beta pricing relation, without an explicit utility function. Critiques to keep in your pocket (pp. 167–172): the market portfolio is not observable (wealth includes human capital and housing), so CAPM tests are tests of a proxy (p. 169). The ICAPM can be accused of giving a ”fishing license” (p. 170): with enough state variables, any factor can be rationalised. Cochrane's discipline (p. 172): for a factor to affect prices … the average investor must be affected by it, which is why he favours factors tied to recessions and financial distress. Use in thesis: in your capital-adequacy framing, a climate transition factor is justified as a priced state variable only if it moves the marginal utility of the representative investor, i.e. is correlated with bad times. This is the ICAPM argument, with the p. 172 restriction as your defence against the fishing-license criticism.\n\n1.6 Testing a factor model (Chapter 12)\nThe chapter (p. 229 intro) sets out two regression approaches. Notation: Rᵉ is an excess return. Time-series regression (pp. 230–233). When the factor is itself a return (or traded), run Rᵉᵢₜ = αᵢ + βᵢ fₜ + εᵢₜ for each asset. The model says all alphas are zero. Test jointly with the GRS F-test (Gibbons–Ross–Shanken), which weighs the alphas by the residual covariance matrix and the Sharpe ratio of the factor. The test also has a neat interpretation: it asks"},{"pdf":145,"printed":130,"ch":6,"text":"whether adding the test assets raises the achievable Sharpe ratio, i.e. whether the factor is on the mean–variance frontier. GMM view (pp. 233–235). The same test can be derived as a GMM moment condition E(mRᵉ) = 0, which delivers standard errors that are robust to non-normal or autocorrelated errors. You can cite this when someone worries that normality is needed. Cross-sectional regression (pp. 235 onward). Two passes: (1) estimate betas from timeseries regressions, (2) regress average returns on betas to estimate λ: E(Rᵉᵢ) = βᵢ′λ + αᵢ. Used when the factor is not a traded return (for example a macro or climate-news variable). The pricing errors αᵢ should be jointly zero. Fama–MacBeth procedure (pp. 245–249).\n1. Estimate betas (full sample or rolling window).\n2. At each date t run a cross-sectional regression of returns on betas: Rᵉᵢₜ = βᵢ λₜ + αᵢₜ.\n3. Average the λₜ over time to get λ̂, and use the time-series standard deviation of λ̂ₜ to get its standard error (divide the variance by T). The logic of why it works (p. 246): the sampling variation of an estimate across samples can be learned from how it varies across subperiods. Quotable (p. 246): ”Sampling error is, after all, about how a statistic would vary from one sample to the next if we repeated the observations.”\n\nTwo cautions that matter for a thesis that uses panel data on banks:\n• Cross-sectional correlation (pp. 247–248): if you pool firms and years and ignore that errors are correlated across firms in the same year, OLS standard errors are far too small. Cochrane: ”the standard errors so computed are often off by a factor of 10” (p. 248). If the regressors do not change over time, Fama–MacBeth, a cross-sectional regression on averages, and pooled OLS with a correction are numerically the same (the proposition on p. 248).\n• Generated regressors (p. 249): Fama–MacBeth does not correct for the fact that betas are estimated. Use the Shanken correction and check it is not large. Also, with corporate-finance panels, errors are likely correlated over time as well as across firms, so use GMM-based errors that handle both.\n\n1.7 What the evidence says (Chapter 20.3, pp. 449–453)\nThis is the part to use when you need a one-paragraph summary of two decades of empirical asset pricing.\n• Prices carry information about expected returns: low price-to-dividend, price-to-earnings or book-to-market signals high future returns, because discount rates move more than cash-flow expectations.\n• Magnifying glasses (p. 449): small high-frequency predictability becomes large at long horizons for slow-moving predictors, and sorting into portfolios makes small effects economically big.\n• Dogs that did not bark (pp. 449–450): dividends do not forecast with the sign they would need if returns were unpredictable, so the variation must be in expected returns."},{"pdf":146,"printed":131,"ch":6,"text":"• The patterns point to premia for recession and financial-distress risk that is not market risk (p. 449, p. 450). Quotable (p. 451): ”We have to get used to the fact that most returns and price variation come from variation in risk premia, not variation in expected cash flows, interest rates, etc.” Quotable (p. 449): ”Prices reveal slow-moving market expectations of subsequent excess returns, because potential offsetting events seem sluggish or absent.”\n\nDoubts (pp. 452–453). Momentum lacks a macro-risk story; premia often shrink after being published or after funds exploit them (the small-firm effect ”completely disappeared in 1980”, p. 452). Cochrane's phrase for the instability: ”In the entire history of finance there has always been an anomaly-du-jour, and momentum is it right now” (p. 452). The CAPM allows extra common movement, but not extra average return for it (p. 453). Use in thesis: a climate or transition-risk premium has to pass the same three tests: (1) is it tied to bad states (so the sign is justified), (2) does it survive publication and out-of-sample, (3) are standard errors corrected for cross-sectional correlation.\n\nFile flag. The PDF name says 2005; the text I used is the revised edition's printed pagination (553 pp.). Cite the printed pages above, not PDF page numbers. Chapter 20.1–20.2 and Chapter 21 detail were not read, so I have not summarised the long-horizon regressions or the consumption-based solutions to the puzzle.\n\n2. MacKinlay (1997), Event Studies in Economics and Finance: reading the file and writing the notes What was read. All 30 PDF pages, cover to references. The paper is A. Craig MacKinlay, ”Event Studies in Economics and Finance”, Journal of Economic Literature 35 (March 1997), pp. 13–39. File flag: your file name says 1996; the paper itself is dated 1997, so cite 1997. Scan flag: the PDF is an image with no text layer, so I OCR'd it. Equations and the numbers in tables 1 and 2 may carry OCR slips, so any figure you quote should be checked against the PDF image. Page numbers below are the journal's printed pages (PDF page 1 = p. 13).\n\n2.1 What an event study is for\nAn event study measures how a specific event changes the value of a firm, using security prices over a short period around the event. Quotable (p. 13): ”given rationality in the marketplace, the effects of an event will be reflected immediately in security prices.”\n\nThat one assumption (informationally efficient prices) is the whole justification. A short window of stock returns replaces months of waiting for accounting or productivity data. Applications named on pp. 13–14: mergers, earnings announcements, debt and equity issues, macro announcements, and changes in the regulatory environment (Schwert 1981). The modern method comes from Ball and Brown (1968) and Fama et al. (1969) (p. 14)."},{"pdf":147,"printed":132,"ch":6,"text":"2.2 The seven-step procedure (Section 2, pp. 14–16)\n1. Define the event and the event window. Make the window longer than the one\nday of interest, normally at least the announcement day and the next day, because news released after the close shows up the next day (p. 15). Pre-event days let you check for leakage.\n2. Select the firms (data availability, industry) and describe the sample, noting any selection bias (p. 15).\n3. Measure the abnormal return: actual return minus the normal return the firm would have had without the event.\n4. Estimate the normal-return model on an estimation window that comes before\nthe event window and does not overlap it, for example the 120 days before the event (p. 15).\n5. Choose the test and the aggregation (null hypothesis, how to combine firms and days).\n6. Present results and diagnostics. Check whether one or two firms drive everything (p. 15).\n7. Interpret: separate competing explanations (p. 16).\nQuotable (p. 15), the definition to cite: ”The abnormal return is the actual ex post return of the security over the event window minus the normal return of the firm over the event window.” Formally, ARᵢₜ = Rᵢₜ − E(Rᵢₜ | Xₜ) (eq. 1).\n\n2.3 Normal-return models (Section 4, pp. 17–19)\n• Constant mean return: Rᵢₜ = μᵢ + ζᵢₜ (eq. 2). Simple, and Brown and Warner (1980, 1985) find it often gives results similar to fancier models (p. 17).\n• Market model: Rᵢₜ = αᵢ + βᵢ Rₘₜ + εᵢₜ (eq. 3, p. 18). It strips out the part of the return explained by the market, so the abnormal return has lower variance and the test is sharper. The gain depends on the regression R². Popular market proxies: S&P 500, CRSP value- or equal-weighted index (p. 18).\n• Other statistical models: multifactor models (industry index, size-matched portfolio). Gains are limited because extra factors add little beyond the market factor, except when all the sample firms share a characteristic such as one industry, which is exactly the case for banks (p. 18).\n• Market-adjusted model: a restricted market model with α = 0 and β = 1, used only when no estimation window exists, for example IPOs (pp. 18–19). MacKinlay's advice: use restricted models only if necessary.\n• Economic models (CAPM, APT) (p. 19): use of the CAPM in event studies ”has\nalmost ceased” because known CAPM deviations make the restriction risky and the plain market model avoids that at little cost. APT-motivated models add little because the first factor behaves like the market. The statistical models assume returns are jointly normal and i.i.d. (p. 17). MacKinlay says the assumption is strong but inference is robust to departures, and that a GMM approach makes the analysis autocorrelation- and heteroskedasticity-consistent."},{"pdf":148,"printed":133,"ch":6,"text":"Connection to Cochrane (Section 1 of this doc): the market model is the one-factor beta model; a multifactor normal-return model is a factor model m = a + b′f. If you add a climate factor to the normal-return model, you are doing a Cochrane-style factor model inside an event study.\n\n2.4 Mechanics: windows, estimation, aggregation (Section 5, pp. 19–24)\nNotation (p. 19, Figure 1 on p. 20). Event date t = 0. Estimation window T₀+1 to T₁, length L₁. Event window T₁+1 to T₂, length L₂. Optional post-event window T₂+1 to T₃. Why windows do not overlap (p. 20): if the event returns enter the estimation, the normalreturn parameters absorb part of the event effect and the abnormal return understates it. Estimation (eqs. 4–6, p. 20): OLS of the stock return on the market over the estimation window. OLS is consistent and efficient under the assumptions. Abnormal return (eq. 7): ARᵢₜ = Rᵢₜ − α̂ᵢ − β̂ᵢ Rₘₜ. Quotable (p. 20): ”The abnormal return is the disturbance term of the market model calculated on an out of sample basis.”\n\nVariance (eq. 8, p. 21): σ²(ARᵢₜ) = σ²ε + a term for sampling error in α̂, β̂. With a long estimation window the second term vanishes, so the abnormal returns are about independent over time with variance σ²ε. Under H₀ (no effect on mean or variance), AR ~ N(0, σ²) (eq. 9). Cumulative abnormal return (CAR) (eqs. 10–12, p. 21): CARᵢ(τ₁,τ₂) = sum of ARᵢₜ from τ₁ to τ₂; its variance is (τ₂ − τ₁ + 1) σ²ε for a long estimation window. Across firms (eqs. 13–20, pp. 22–24): average AR across N firms, then cumulate over time (or cumulate by firm then average, which is equivalent, eq. 17). If event windows do not overlap, covariances are zero and the variance of the average is the average of variances divided by N. The test statistic is the CAR divided by its standard error, asymptotically standard normal (eq. 20, p. 24). Patell (1976) suggests standardising each abnormal return first.\n\n2.5 MacKinlay's worked example (Sections 3 and 5.D, pp. 16–17, 24–27)\nDesign: 30 Dow Jones firms, 1989–1993, 600 quarterly earnings announcements, split by I/B/E/S surprise into good news (more than 2.5% above expectation), no news and bad news (189 / 173 / 238 events). Daily returns, 41-day event window (20 days before, day 0, 20 after), 250-day estimation window (p. 17). Results (pp. 24–26):\n• Day 0 average abnormal return: +0.965% for good news (standard error 0.104%, test statistic 9.28) and −0.679% for bad news (standard error 0.098%, statistic −6.93). Nonews firms: −0.091%, under one standard error from zero.\n• Day 1 effect (+0.251% and −0.204%) is explained by announcements made after the close.\n• Constant-mean model gives the same conclusion but larger standard errors (0.130 / 0.124 / 0.131 against 0.104 / 0.098 / 0.098). This is the variance reduction from the market model in action."},{"pdf":149,"printed":134,"ch":6,"text":"• The CAR plots show the market learns gradually: good-news firms drift up and bad-news firms drift down in days −20 to −1 (p. 26), i.e. partial anticipation.\n\n2.6 Clustering (Section 5.E, pp. 27–28) ← crucial for your bank sample\nIf event windows overlap in calendar time, abnormal returns are cross-correlated and the variance formula in 2.4 is wrong (Bernard 1987). Two fixes:\n1. Portfolio approach: aggregate the firms into one portfolio in event time and run the single-series test. This allows for cross-correlation.\n2. Multivariate regression with event-date dummies (Schipper and Thompson 1983, 1985; Collins and Dent 1984): needed when there is total clustering, the same event on the same day for many firms. It allows some firms to move up and others down, but the test often has poor finite-sample properties and low power (p. 28).\n\n2.7 Relaxing the null and testing risk (Section 6, pp. 28–29)\nIf the event also raises variance, estimating variance from the pre-event window understates it. Fix: use the cross-section of CARs to estimate the variance, equation 21 (Boehmer, Musumeci and Poulsen 1991). It requires no cross-sectional correlation, which is again a no-clustering condition, but not homoskedasticity. To test for a change in risk, let the market-model beta shift over the event window (Kane and Unal 1988).\n\n2.8 Power (Section 7, pp. 28–31)\nPower is the probability of rejecting the null when the abnormal return is really non-zero. MacKinlay tabulates it for a 5% two-sided test (Table 2, p. 29), for abnormal returns of 0.5%, 1.0%, 1.5%, 2.0%, and for two values of the CAR standard deviation: 2% (variance 0.0004; one-day window, no variance increase) and 4% (variance 0.0016; multi-day window or event raises variance). Useful numbers from his discussion (pp. 30–31):\n• With a 2% standard deviation and a 0.5% abnormal return, power with 20 firms is only 0.20, and you need more than 60 firms to reach 0.50.\n• With a 2.0% abnormal return and 20 firms, power is 0.99.\n• With a 4% standard deviation, a 0.5% abnormal return gives power 0.09 with 20 firms and 0.42 with 200 firms; with a 1.5% abnormal return and 30 firms, 0.54. Quotable (p. 31): if power is not sufficient, ”search for ways of increasing the power. This can be done by increasing the sample size, shortening the event window, or by developing more specific predictions to test.” (The sentence runs from p. 31 to p. 32; quote it as one passage.)\n\n2.9 Nonparametric tests (Section 8, p. 32)\n• Sign test: under H₀ the proportion of positive CARs is 0.5; statistic (eq. 23) = [N⁺/N − 0.5] √N / 0.5 ~ N(0,1). Weakness: misspecified when abnormal returns are skewed (daily data)."},{"pdf":150,"printed":135,"ch":6,"text":"• Corrado (1989) rank test: rank each firm's abnormal returns across the window; the event-day rank has expected value (L₂+1)/2 under H₀ (eq. 24). Robust to skewness. Use them alongside the parametric test as a robustness check, not instead. Campbell and Wasley (1993) found the rank test more reliable than parametric tests for NASDAQ daily returns (p. 32).\n\n2.10 Cross-sectional regressions of abnormal returns (Section 9, pp. 33–34)\nRegress each event's AR (or CAR) on firm characteristics: ARⱼ = δ₀ + δ₁ x₁ⱼ + … + ηⱼ (eq. 26). Use White heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors because residuals will not be homoskedastic (p. 33). Example: Asquith and Mullins (1986) regressed the two-day CAR for equity-issue announcements on offering size and prior CAR. Pitfall (p. 33): characteristics can predict how much of the event was anticipated, which creates selection bias and inconsistent OLS. Solutions: model the selection explicitly (Acharya 1988; Eckbo et al. 1990); Prabhala (1995) argues OLS with weak conditions still gives tstatistics that are lower bounds on significance.\n\n2.11 Other issues (Section 10, pp. 34–36)\n• Sampling interval (p. 34): shorter is much better for power. With 50 securities and a 1% abnormal return, power is 0.94 with daily data, 0.35 weekly, 0.12 monthly.\n• Event-date uncertainty (p. 35): widen the window to days 0 and +1; Ball and Torous (1988) show the informal fix works well.\n• Robustness (p. 35): normality matters only for exact finite-sample results; convergence is fast (Brown and Warner 1985).\n• Biases (pp. 35–36): nonsynchronous trading (adjust beta by Scholes–Williams; for liquid stocks the effect is small), and an upward bias in CARs for illiquid stocks with wide bid– ask spreads (Blume and Stambaugh 1983); fix by using buy-and-hold returns.\n\n2.12 Conclusion and limits (Section 11, pp. 36–38)\nSuccesses: mergers (targets earn large positive abnormal returns, over 20% in Jarrell and Poulsen's 663-takeover sample; acquirers about zero), and external financing (equity issues about −2.7% to −3.6% over two days; debt issues about −0.23%), which motivated the peckingorder theory. Courts have implicitly accepted the method (Basic v. Levinson, 1988) (p. 37). Where it works poorly, and why this matters to you: Quotable (p. 37), regulatory events: ”the wealth effects of regulatory changes for affected entities can be difficult to detect using event study methodology. The problem is that regulatory changes are often debated in the political arena over time and any accompanying wealth effects generally will gradually be incorporated into the value of a corporation as the probability of the change being adopted increases.” Quotable (p. 38), closing line: ”event studies have shown that, as would be expected in a rational marketplace, prices do respond to new information.”"},{"pdf":151,"printed":136,"ch":6,"text":"2.13 Using this in your thesis\nIf your design studies how public-sector bank stock prices react to climate-policy or climateregulation announcements (for example international climate agreements or RBI/SEBI disclosure and stress-testing circulars), MacKinlay tells you what to worry about:\n• Total clustering. Every bank gets the same news on the same day, so the standard N-independent-firms test is invalid (pp. 27–28). Use the portfolio approach, or the dummy-variable multivariate regression, and say so explicitly.\n• Low power with a small sample. You will have about a dozen public-sector banks. From Table 2 (p. 29, read from OCR) with 12 firms: at a 2% CAR standard deviation, a 1% abnormal return is detected with power about 0.41; at a 4% standard deviation, about 0.14. Report power, use daily data and short windows, and do not read a non-rejection as no effect (pp. 30–32).\n• Anticipation and slow-moving policy. MacKinlay's own warning about regulatory events (p. 37) applies. Consider dating the event as the days when the probability of adoption changed (Schipper and Thompson approach, p. 38), and show pre-event CARs.\n• Normal return model. The market model is the default (p. 19). With banks, an industry/banking index as an additional factor is the case MacKinlay says is worth the multifactor model (p. 18).\n• Second stage. Regress CARs on bank characteristics, for example capital adequacy, brown exposure, NPA ratio, with White standard errors (p. 33), and keep the anticipation caveat in mind.\n• Robustness. Add the Corrado rank or sign test alongside the parametric test (p. 32). What was not read: the cited papers (Brown and Warner 1980/1985, Corrado 1989, Schipper and Thompson, Boehmer et al., Prabhala). Details of those tests are as MacKinlay summarises them.\n\n3. Survey-based folder: Stroebel and Wurgler (2021), the working\npaper survey and the supplement\nWhat was read. All three files in full: the published paper (Stroebel and Wurgler, ”What do you think about climate finance?”, Journal of Financial Economics 142 (2021), pp. 487–498, 12 pp.), the NBER working-paper version (No. 29136, August 2021, 26 pp.) and the Internet Appendix (5 pp.). Page numbers below are the journal's printed pages (487–498). Flags: the Internet Appendix figures A1 (the survey invitation) and A2 (example questions) are images and were not read; I read its tables A1 and A2 only. For the working paper I compared the text, the Table 3 numbers and the conclusion against the published version; I did not check every cell of every table.\n\n3.1 What this paper is, and what it is not\nIt is an opinion survey of finance academics, finance professionals and public-sector economists about climate finance. It reports what experts believe about climate risks and asset prices, not evidence about what prices actually do. That distinction is the first thing to say when you cite it."},{"pdf":152,"printed":137,"ch":6,"text":"Quotable (abstract, p. 487): ”By an overwhelming margin, respondents believe that asset prices underestimate climate risks rather than overestimate them.”\n\nWhy it belongs in the layer on asset pricing: it is the survey-based counterpart of Cochrane's question. Cochrane asks whether risks that covary with bad states are priced; Stroebel and Wurgler ask the experts whether climate risk is priced enough and whether climate risk covaries with good or bad times. It is also written by two of the people behind the Giglio–Kelly–Stroebel review in Layer 6.\n\n3.2 Survey design (Section 2, pp. 488–489)\n• Population targeted: (i) professors at the top 100 finance departments, ranked by publications in the Journal of Finance, JFE and RFS in 2010–2020 (3,570 email addresses); (ii) NYU Stern graduates working in finance (6,921); (iii) researchers and policymakers at 17 public institutions, among them central banks, the IMF and the World Bank (955). Total 11,446 email addresses (p. 488).\n• Instrument: a Qualtrics online survey, one recruitment email per person, anonymous, 5–7 minutes, run in July 2021. Answer options were randomly ordered (table notes, pp. 493–497).\n• Response: 861 complete responses, a 7.5% response rate. Faculty 453 (12.7% of those contacted), practitioners 294 (4.2%), regulators/public sector 72 (7.5%); 42 did not give an occupation (p. 488). The authors compare this with 9% for the Graham–Harvey CFO survey and 4.3% for McCahery et al. (p. 488).\n• Who answered (Table 1, p. 490): 55% faculty, 36% private sector, 9% public sector; 72% North America, 14% Europe, 7% Asia, 7% rest of world; 69% say they personally are concerned about climate change; 59% work on climate finance; 45% received their highest degree in 2010 or later.\n• Sample-selection bias, admitted by the authors:\nQuotable (p. 488): ”Respondents are probably more interested in climate finance than nonrespondents.”\n\nThey argue this is ”not entirely unhelpful” because you may prefer the views of the most informed, and they check whether answers differ by climate concern and professional interest (p. 488–489). The Internet Appendix adds 158 respondents who found the survey on Twitter and LinkedIn and reports that results are broadly similar (footnote 6, p. 488).\n\n3.3 Findings, with the numbers\nA. Which climate risks matter most? (Section 3.1, p. 489; Table 2, p. 493). Respondents ranked five risks, 1 = most important: Table 6.2. Survey-based folder: Stroebel and Wurgler (2021): 3.3 Findings, with the numbers Risk\n\nNext 5 years (average rank)\n\nNext 30 years (average rank)\n\nRegulatory\n\n1.9\n\n2.6\n\nStakeholder (customers, employees)\n\n2.9\n\n3.5\n\nPhysical\n\n3.1\n\n2.2"},{"pdf":153,"printed":138,"ch":6,"text":"Risk\n\nNext 5 years (average rank)\n\nNext 30 years (average rank)\n\nTechnological\n\n3.4\n\n3.0\n\nLegal\n\n3.6\n\n3.7\n\nRegulatory (transition) risk is the top short-run risk, ”ranked a full position higher, on average, than the second-most-cited risk” (p. 489). Physical risk is the top 30-year risk. Public-sector respondents already rated physical risk second over five years (p. 489). The exception to physical-first at 30 years is the low-concern group. B. Are asset prices right? (Section 3.2, p. 489–491; Table 3, p. 494). Pooled shares: Table 6.3. Survey-based folder: Stroebel and Wurgler (2021): 3.3 Findings, with the numbers (b) Market\n\nToo much\n\nCorrect\n\nNot enough\n\nNo opinion\n\nStock markets\n\n21%\n\n60%\n\n16%\n\nReal estate\n\n17%\n\n67%\n\n15%\n\nInsurance\n\n25%\n\n42%\n\n30%\n\n”Not enough” outnumbers ”too much” 20 to 1 for stocks (60:3), 67 to 1 for real estate and 21 to 1 for insurance (42:2) (p. 489). It holds in every subgroup, even among the low-concern respondents. Differences: private-sector respondents (73%) are 22 points more likely than academics (51%) to say stocks underprice climate risk, ”perhaps a result of a stronger belief among finance academics in the efficiency of markets” (p. 491). Those who think prices are too low rank physical risk higher (p. 491). Quotable (p. 491): ”Either the widespread belief that asset prices and insurance markets insufficiently price climate risk is way off, or these markets have a lot catching up to do.”\n\nC. Do climate payoffs come in good or bad times, and what discount rate? (Section 3.3, pp. 491–492; Table 4, p. 495).\n• A mitigation project pays off: good times 32%, bad times 13%, equally in both 55%.\n• Median social discount rate for a project with certain benefits 50 years out: 4%; with uncertain benefits: 7%; so the median risk premium is 3% (public sector 1%, private sector 4%). The authors frame the theory (p. 491). Climate change is modelled either as a tax on consumption that grows with output (Nordhaus 2008) or as a disaster that triggers a downturn (Barro 2013; Weitzman 2012, 2014). Quotable (p. 491), the pricing logic: ”If climate change is more costly in good economic times, then investments to mitigate climate change pay off disproportionately in those times and deserve a positive risk premium. On the other hand, if investments to mitigate climate change pay of largely in bad economic times, they should be considered hedges that command a negative risk premium.”\n\nThis is Cochrane's p = E(x)/Rᶠ + cov(m, x) in words: a payoff that comes in good times (low m) is worth less than its expected value, so it must be discounted at a higher rate. Respondents who say ”good times” are three times more numerous than ”bad times”, so the median respondent"},{"pdf":154,"printed":139,"ch":6,"text":"applies a positive premium (pp. 491–492). The authors note the question forced a single static rate (footnote 11, p. 491). D. What will force firms to change? (Section 3.4, p. 492; Table 5, p. 496). Share choosing each in their top three: carbon taxes 52%, institutional investors 48%, government subsidies 43%, customers 41%, non-financial regulation 27%, financial regulation 22%, banks and creditors 16%, employees 6%, individual investors 5%, voluntary corporate action 0% (p. 492: ”not one respondent was optimistic that voluntary behavior by corporations … would be a significant force”). Private-sector respondents prefer institutional investors and customers; faculty and public sector prefer carbon taxes. E. What should research work on? (Section 3.5, pp. 492 and 498; Table 6, p. 497). Top three: effects of government incentives (35%), pricing of climate risk in financial markets (34%), climate change effect on systemic risk (28%; 47% among public-sector respondents), real effects of socially responsible investing (23%), new financial instruments (21%). Stress tests: 13%. The authors compare these priorities with 420 climate-finance papers uploaded to SSRN-FEN over three years: the rank correlation is 0.85, 0.91 for those who work on climate finance and 0.64 for outsiders (p. 498). But some topics are under-researched relative to stated importance, among them systemic risk (28% of respondents vs 15% of SSRN papers) and stress tests (13% vs 4%) (Table 6, p. 497; text p. 498).\n\n3.4 Working paper vs published version\nThe NBER working paper (No. 29136, August 2021) and the JFE article give the same survey, the same sample and the same results. The differences I found are minor: the working paper lists extra JEL codes (G32, Q56); its introduction says ”There was a strong response to our survey request” where the journal text opens with the uniformity of opinion; a few reference years differ (for example Bansal, Kiku and Ochoa 2017 vs 2018 in the body text); and the working paper has ”[INSERT TABLE n NEAR HERE]” markers with the tables at the end. Cite the published article.\n\n3.5 What the supplement adds (Internet Appendix, tables A1 and A2)\n• Extended sample: adds the Twitter/LinkedIn respondents and splits climate concern into four levels: very high (33%), high (37%), low (26%), no concern (4%).\n• The ”no concern” group answers very differently (Table A2). Over five years they rank physical risk 4.6 (vs 3.1 pooled); only 8% say stocks underprice climate risk and 22% say ”too much”; their median discount rate for risk-free benefits is 7% and for uncertain benefits 17% (a 10-point premium). This is a small group (about 4% of the sample), but it shows the ”consensus” is concentrated among people who think climate change is real and serious.\n• High-concern respondents who work in climate finance are the most convinced prices are too low (82% for stocks).\n\n3.6 Using this in your thesis\n• Support for the transition-risk framing. Experts rank regulatory (transition) risk first over five years and physical risk first over 30 years (Table 2, p. 493). That matches a"},{"pdf":155,"printed":140,"ch":6,"text":"thesis horizon of capital adequacy over the next few years in banks exposed to transition risk. Use it as expert-opinion support, not as evidence.\n• Market underpricing as motivation. Cite the 60:3 and 20-to-1 numbers (p. 489) as evidence that experts expect climate risk is not fully priced, which is the reason a bank-capital or event-study analysis might find reactions to climate news. Pair it with Cochrane on why a premium need not exist for idiosyncratic risk.\n• Gap in the literature. Systemic risk and stress tests are rated far more important than the volume of research on them (Table 6, p. 497). For a thesis on capital adequacy this is a ready-made sentence in the introduction. Public-sector respondents rate systemic risk at 47%.\n• Discount rate for climate risk. The 4% / 7% / 3% medians (Table 4, p. 495) are a reference point if you discuss how to value long-dated climate losses on bank balance sheets.\n• Limits to state: 72% of respondents are from North America and only about 7% (roughly 60 people) from Asia; none are India-specific in the tabulations I read; practitioners are largely NYU Stern alumni; response rate 7.5%; it was run in July 2021; it measures belief, not outcomes (p. 488). If you quote it for the Indian context, say so. Quotable (conclusion, p. 498): ”Scientists often describe climate change with superlatives. Urgent. Dire. Existential. The superlatives are all bad.” and ”As with other aspects of climate change, time will tell whether these beliefs are justified.”\n\n4. Master quotables list and how Layer 5 feeds the thesis\n4.1 The quotables, ranked by usefulness\nPage numbers are printed pages of each source. Quote exactly as shown and add the page when you cite. Table 6.4. Master quotables list and how Layer 5 feeds the thesis: 4.1 The quotables, ranked by usefulness\n\nSource and\npage\n\nQuote\n\nUse it to\n\nCochrane, p.\n\n”All asset pricing models amount to\nalternative ways of connecting the\nstochastic discount factor to data.”\n\nSay that CAPM, ICAPM, APT and\na climate factor are all versions of\none idea\n\nCochrane, p.\n151\n\n”all factor models are derived as\nspecializations of the\nconsumption-based model.”\n\nJustify why a factor model\n(including a climate factor) has a\ntheoretical basis\n\nCochrane, p.\n451\n\n”We have to get used to the fact that\nArgue that climate repricing can\nmost returns and price variation come\nshow up as a change in risk premia,\nfrom variation in risk premia, not\nnot in expected cash flows\nvariation in expected cash flows, interest\nrates, etc.”"},{"pdf":156,"printed":141,"ch":6,"text":"Source and\npage\n\nQuote\n\nUse it to\n\nCochrane, p.\n449\n\n”Prices reveal slow-moving market\nexpectations of subsequent excess\nreturns, because potential offsetting\nevents seem sluggish or absent.”\n\nSummarise the empirical\nasset-pricing evidence in one line\n\nCochrane, p.\n248\n\n”the standard errors so computed are\noften off by a factor of 10” (about\npooled regressions that ignore\ncross-sectional correlation)\n\nJustify clustered or Fama–MacBeth\nstandard errors\n\nMacKinlay,\np. 13\n\n”given rationality in the marketplace,\nthe effects of an event will be reflected\nimmediately in security prices.”\n\nState the assumption behind any\nevent study\n\nMacKinlay,\np. 15\n\n”The abnormal return is the actual ex\npost return of the security over the\nevent window minus the normal return\nof the firm over the event window.”\n\nDefine abnormal return in your\nmethodology chapter\n\nMacKinlay,\np. 37\n\n”the wealth effects of regulatory changes Acknowledge the limit of event\nfor affected entities can be difficult to\nstudies for policy events, and justify\ndetect using event study methodology.” a design that dates probability\nchanges\n\nStroebel and\nWurgler, p.\n487\n\n”By an overwhelming margin,\nrespondents believe that asset prices\nunderestimate climate risks rather than\noverestimate them.”\n\nMotivate the question of whether\nclimate risk is priced\n\nStroebel and\nWurgler, p.\n488\n\n”Respondents are probably more\ninterested in climate finance than\nnonrespondents.”\n\nState the survey's selection bias\nwhen you cite it\n\nStroebel and\nWurgler, p.\n491\n\n”If climate change is more costly in\ngood economic times, then investments\nto mitigate climate change pay off\ndisproportionately in those times and\ndeserve a positive risk premium.”\n\nLink climate risk to the sign of a\nrisk premium (Cochrane's\ncovariance logic)\n\nStroebel and\nWurgler, p.\n491\n\n”Either the widespread belief that asset Frame the underpricing hypothesis\nfairly\nprices and insurance markets\ninsufficiently price climate risk is way\noff, or these markets have a lot catching\nup to do.”\n\nNumbers worth keeping. Experts' average ranks of regulatory risk over 5 years, 1.9 (Table 2, p. 493); stocks underpriced 60% vs overpriced 3% (Table 3, p. 494); median discount rate 4% risk-free, 7% for climate mitigation, premium 3% (Table 4, p. 495); systemic risk chosen by 28% of respondents (47% of the public sector) but 15% of SSRN papers (Table 6, p. 497); event-study power with 12 firms and a 1% abnormal return: about 0.41 at a 2% standard deviation and 0.14 at 4% (MacKinlay Table 2, p. 29, from OCR).\n\n4.2 How Layer 5 feeds the thesis\nThe chain of logic."},{"pdf":157,"printed":142,"ch":6,"text":"1. Cochrane gives the theory: a risk that is correlated with bad states earns a premium; a diversifiable one does not. A climate factor is credible only if it moves marginal utility (p. 172) and survives tests with correct standard errors (pp. 245–249).\n2. MacKinlay gives the empirical tool to see whether and when markets repriced a climaterelated event, and the checklist of what can go wrong: clustering, anticipation, low power with few banks.\n3. Stroebel and Wurgler give the expert-belief evidence that motivates both: markets are thought to underprice climate risk, regulation is the near-term risk, and systemic risk is under-researched. Link to Layer 4 (financial contagion and systemic risk). Layer 4 explains how a shock to some banks spreads through balance sheets (Eisenberg–Noe, Acemoglu et al.) and how liquidity spirals amplify it (Brunnermeier–Pedersen). Layer 5 explains how markets price such a shock and how you would detect the repricing. Stroebel and Wurgler's Table 6 says systemic risk from climate is wanted by researchers and regulators but under-supplied. Link to Layer 6 (climate finance and central-bank view). Layer 6 covers the Green Swan, Carney's tragedy-of-the-horizon speech, the Giglio–Kelly–Stroebel review and applied stress-testing work. Read it with this layer in hand: when those papers talk about climate risk being ”priced”, ”underpriced” or a ”factor”, they are using the Cochrane vocabulary in Section 1; when they use event-based evidence, they are using MacKinlay's. The Stroebel–Wurgler survey is by the same research group as the review, so it also tells you what that group thinks is still open. Where to use each in the thesis.\n• Literature review: Cochrane p. 7 and p. 151 (theory), Stroebel and Wurgler p. 487 (belief evidence).\n• Methodology chapter: MacKinlay pp. 15–24 (definitions and tests), pp. 27–28 (clustering), pp. 28–31 (power); Cochrane pp. 245–249 (Fama–MacBeth) if you run crosssectional regressions.\n• Limitations chapter: MacKinlay p. 37 (regulatory events), Stroebel and Wurgler p. 488 (selection), Cochrane pp. 452–453 (premia can vanish).\n\n4.3 What was and was not read\n• Cochrane: read in full Chapters 1 (pp. 3–24) and 9 (pp. 149–174), Chapter 12 (pp. 229–249, up to the Fama–MacBeth discussion) and Chapter 20.3 (pp. 449–453). Not read: the remaining ~460 pages, including GMM theory, options, bonds, long-horizon regressions (20.1–20.2) and the consumption-model chapters (21 onwards).\n• MacKinlay: read in full from OCR. Check equation numbers and table values in the PDF before quoting them.\n• Stroebel and Wurgler: published paper, working paper and Internet Appendix tables read; Internet Appendix figures A1–A2 (images) not read.\n• The works these papers cite were not read."},{"pdf":158,"printed":143,"ch":6,"text":"4.4 File flags\n1. The MacKinlay file name says 1996; the paper is Journal of Economic Literature 35 (March 1997), pp. 13–39. Cite 1997.\n2. The MacKinlay PDF is a scan without a text layer, which is why some table values come from OCR.\n3. Cochrane is the revised edition's printed pagination; use these printed page numbers, not PDF page numbers.\n4. The survey paper has an NBER working-paper twin; cite the JFE article (2021, 142, 487–498). Next: Layer 6 builds directly on this one."},{"pdf":159,"printed":144,"ch":6,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 5"},{"pdf":160,"printed":145,"ch":6,"text":""},{"pdf":161,"printed":146,"ch":6,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 5"},{"pdf":162,"printed":147,"ch":6,"text":""}],"cards":[]};

window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.ln=window.CHTML.ln||{};window.CHTML.ln[6]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Before you start: concepts needed (stochastic discount factor, returns and risk premia, abnormal returns, survey methods), a map of the readings, and suggested reading order</h1>\n<p data-b=\"2\">This layer has three very different readings: a textbook (Cochrane), a methods paper (MacKinlay) and an opinion survey (Stroebel and Wurgler). They answer one question from three directions: <em>how would you know whether climate risk is priced, and what do experts think?</em> You can follow these notes without having read the originals. The notes below assume only the ideas on this page.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">Concepts you need first</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"4\"><strong>Return and excess return.</strong> A return is (end price + payoff) / start price. An excess return subtracts the risk-free rate. Everything in asset pricing is about why excess returns differ across assets.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"5\"><strong>Risk premium, beta, alpha.</strong> A <em>risk premium</em> is the average excess return an investor demands for bearing a risk. A <em>beta</em> measures how much an asset moves with a risk factor (a regression slope). An <em>alpha</em> is the average return left over after the factor explains what it can; a good model has alphas near zero.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"6\"><strong>Covariance and the idea that only some risk is paid.</strong> An asset that does badly exactly when you are poor (a recession, a crisis) is unattractive and must offer a higher average return. Risk that you can diversify away earns nothing.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"7\"><strong>Stochastic discount factor (SDF), m.</strong> One number per state of the world that says how much a dollar is worth in that state. Price = expected (m × payoff). High m means \"bad times, a dollar is precious\". You do not need the utility theory behind it, only this reading of m.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"8\"><strong>Regression basics.</strong> What an OLS slope, a residual, an R², a t-statistic and a standard error are, and why errors that are correlated across firms make standard errors too small.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"9\"><strong>Abnormal return.</strong> The return on a stock beyond what it would have earned without a specific event. MacKinlay’ s whole method rests on this.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"10\"><strong>Survey vocabulary.</strong> <em>Response rate</em> (completed / invited), <em>selection bias</em> (people who answer differ from people who do not), <em>ranking vs choose-up-to-three questions</em>, <em>subgroup breakdowns</em>.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"11\" id=\"b11\">Map of the readings</h2>\n<table data-b=\"12\">\n<caption><strong>Table 6.1.</strong> Before you start: Map of the readings</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Reading</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What it is</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">The question it answers</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it feeds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cochrane, <em>Asset Pricing</em> (2005), selected chapters</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Graduate textbook on one idea: price = expected discounted payoff</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why do assets earn different returns, and how do you test a pricing model?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Theory and test design for any climate factor; standard errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">MacKinlay (1997), <em>Event Studies in Economics and Finance</em></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Methods survey, JEL</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">How do you measure the price effect of a specific announcement, and how reliable is the test?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The method for measuring stock reactions to climate events or rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stroebel and Wurgler (2021), <em>What do you think about climate finance?</em></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Expert opinion survey; working paper and appendix</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">What do finance experts believe about climate risk and whether it is priced?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Motivation and literature gap; discount-rate benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"13\" id=\"b13\">Suggested order</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"14\"><strong>Section 1 (Cochrane), in the order of its own sub-sections.</strong> 1.1 and 1.2 first: they hold the one idea. 1.5 next for factor models, then 1.6 for tests, 1.7 for the evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"15\"><strong>Section 2 (MacKinlay).</strong> Start with 2.2 (the procedure), then 2.3–2.4 (models and tests), then jump to 2.6 (clustering) and 2.8 (power), then 2.13 for your thesis.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"16\"><strong>Section 3 (Stroebel and Wurgler).</strong> Read the tables in 3.3, then 3.6.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"17\"><strong>Section 4.</strong> The quotables list and the thesis links. If you only have ten minutes, read 4.1 and 4.2.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"18\" id=\"b18\">What this layer connects to</h2>\n<p data-b=\"19\">It comes after Layer 4 (contagion and systemic risk) because those papers describe how shocks spread; this layer describes how markets price and test for such shocks. It is a prerequisite for <strong>Layer 6</strong> (climate finance and the central-bank view), whose papers use the vocabulary introduced here: priced risk, factor, premium, event-based evidence.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"20\" id=\"b20\">1. Cochrane, Asset Pricing: reading the file and writing the notes</h1>\n<p data-b=\"21\"><strong>What was read.</strong> The file is the 2005 revised edition (553 printed pages). It is a textbook, so I read the parts your thesis can use: Chapter 1 (the core idea, pp. 3–24), Chapter 9 (factor pricing models, pp. 149–174), Chapter 12 (regression tests, pp. 229–249, up to the Fama–MacBeth discussion) and the closing summary of Chapter 20 (pp. 449–453). I did <strong>not</strong> read the rest of the book (GMM theory in Chapters 10–11, option pricing, bonds, the long-horizon material in 20.1–20.2, the equity-premium chapter 21 beyond its opening). Page numbers below are the <strong>printed</strong> book pages, not PDF pages.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"22\" id=\"b22\">1.1 The one idea: price equals expected discounted payoff</h2>\n<p data-b=\"23\">Everything in the book is one equation. An asset with payoff xₜ₊₁ has price</p>\n<p data-b=\"24\"><strong>pₜ = Eₜ(mₜ₊₁ xₜ₊₁)</strong> (the central formula, p. 6)</p>\n<p data-b=\"25\">where m is the <strong>stochastic discount factor (SDF)</strong>. For a consumer with utility u(c) and impatience β, mₜ₊₁ = β u′(cₜ₊₁)/u′(cₜ): the marginal rate of substitution between today and tomorrow (p. 6). Intuition: a payoff is worth more if it arrives in a state where you are hungry (consumption is low, marginal utility high).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"26\"><strong>Quotable (p. 7):</strong> \"All asset pricing models amount to alternative ways of connecting the stochastic discount factor to data.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"27\">This is the sentence to use when you need to say that CAPM, ICAPM, APT, consumption models and your own climate factor are all siblings.</p>\n<p data-b=\"28\"><strong>Returns form</strong> (p. 8): a gross return R = x/p, so 1 = E(mR). <strong>Risk-free rate</strong> (pp. 10–11): Rᶠ = 1/E(m). So E(m) is the price of a sure dollar; when interest rates are high, m is small on average.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"29\" id=\"b29\">1.2 Where risk premia come from</h2>\n<p data-b=\"30\">Using cov(m, x) = E(mx) − E(m)E(x):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"31\"><strong>Price with a risk correction</strong> (p. 13): p = E(x)/Rᶠ + cov(m, x). The first term is the present value for a risk-neutral investor; the covariance term is the risk adjustment. A payoff that is high when m is high (bad times) is worth more than its expected value.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"32\"><strong>Excess return</strong> (p. 14): E(Rⁱ) − Rᶠ = −Rᶠ cov(m, Rⁱ). Assets that pay off badly when m is high (in bad times) must offer a higher average return. This is the formal reason a bank stock or a carbon-heavy firm that collapses in a bad state must earn a premium.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"33\"><strong>Idiosyncratic risk gets no premium</strong> (p. 15): only the part of a payoff that covaries with m is priced. Diversifiable risk is free to ignore. Link to Layer 4: contagion and network losses matter for asset prices only to the extent they load on m.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"34\"><strong>Beta representation</strong> (p. 16): E(Rⁱ) = Rᶠ + βⁱ,ₘ λₘ, where β is the regression coefficient of the return on m and λ is the price of risk. With consumption as the factor, λ is proportional to risk aversion times the variance of consumption growth (p. 17).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"35\" id=\"b35\">1.3 Bounds and the equity-premium puzzle</h2>\n<p data-b=\"36\">Because correlation cannot exceed one, the SDF must be volatile enough to explain observed Sharpe ratios (the <strong>Hansen–Jagannathan-type bound</strong>, eq. 1.17, pp. 17–19):</p>\n<p data-b=\"37\">E(Rⁱ) − Rᶠ / σ(Rⁱ) ≤ σ(m) / E(m).</p>\n<p data-b=\"38\">The left side is the <strong>Sharpe ratio</strong> of any asset; the right side is the volatility of the SDF. Cochrane’ s illustration (pp. 20–21) is that with observed US stock-market Sharpe ratios the required risk aversion in a consumption model is around 50, and about 250 if the consumption-return correlation is only 0.2. That is the <strong>equity premium puzzle</strong>: plausible risk aversion is far smaller.</p>\n<p data-b=\"39\">Why this matters for you: the same logic is used when someone argues that climate risk must be priced (or is not). If investors fear a climate state of the world, m must be high there, and assets that do badly there should earn a premium. The Sharpe-ratio bound also tells you how large any climate premium <em>could</em> be before it becomes implausible.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"40\" id=\"b40\">1.4 Predictability and the random-walk idea</h2>\n<p data-b=\"41\">pp. 22–24: if returns were unpredictable, prices would follow a random walk, but that is not a requirement of asset pricing. Expected returns can vary over time (risk aversion or risk varies), so some predictability is perfectly compatible with rational pricing. Present-value logic (p. 24) connects price to future cash flows: price moves because expected cash flows or discount rates move.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"42\" id=\"b42\">1.5 Factor models: how theory gets to data (Chapter 9)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"43\">Start (pp. 149–151): marginal utility is not observed, so approximate m by a linear function of observable factors: <strong>m = a + b′f</strong>. This is exactly equivalent to a beta-pricing model <strong>E(Rⁱ) = γ + βⁱ′λ</strong> (λ are factor risk premia).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"44\"><strong>Quotable (p. 151):</strong> \"all factor models are derived as specializations of the consumption-based model.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"45\">How each classic model picks the factor:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"46\"><strong>CAPM</strong> (pp. 152–157). The factor is the market return. Cochrane shows four routes to it: quadratic utility, exponential utility with normal returns, a quadratic value function, and log utility (the last uses the market portfolio as the claim on all consumption). Result: E(Rⁱ) − Rᶠ = βⁱ,ₘ <span>[</span>E(Rᵐ) − Rᶠ<span>]</span>. Only market beta is priced.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"47\"><strong>ICAPM</strong> (pp. 165–167). Investors also care about <em>state variables</em> that shift the investment opportunity set (future returns, labour income, recession risk). Extra factors are priced if they hedge those shifts.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"48\"><strong>APT</strong> (pp. 173–174). If returns have a factor structure with small residual risk, no-arbitrage alone pins down an approximate beta pricing relation, without an explicit utility function.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"49\"><strong>Critiques to keep in your pocket</strong> (pp. 167–172): the market portfolio is not observable (wealth includes human capital and housing), so CAPM tests are tests of a proxy (p. 169). The ICAPM can be accused of giving a \"fishing license\" (p. 170): with enough state variables, any factor can be rationalised. Cochrane’ s discipline (p. 172): for a factor to affect prices … the average investor must be affected by it, which is why he favours factors tied to recessions and financial distress.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"50\"><strong>Use in thesis:</strong> in your capital-adequacy framing, a <em>climate transition factor</em> is justified as a priced state variable only if it moves the marginal utility of the representative investor, i.e. is correlated with bad times. This is the ICAPM argument, with the p. 172 restriction as your defence against the fishing-license criticism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"51\" id=\"b51\">1.6 Testing a factor model (Chapter 12)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"52\">The chapter (p. 229 intro) sets out two regression approaches. Notation: Rᵉ is an excess return.</p>\n<p data-b=\"53\"><strong>Time-series regression</strong> (pp. 230–233). When the factor is itself a return (or traded), run Rᵉᵢₜ = αᵢ + βᵢ fₜ + εᵢₜ for each asset. The model says <strong>all alphas are zero</strong>. Test jointly with the <strong>GRS F-test</strong> (Gibbons–Ross–Shanken), which weighs the alphas by the residual covariance matrix and the Sharpe ratio of the factor. The test also has a neat interpretation: it asks whether adding the test assets raises the achievable Sharpe ratio, i.e. whether the factor is on the mean–variance frontier.</p>\n<p data-b=\"54\"><strong>GMM view</strong> (pp. 233–235). The same test can be derived as a GMM moment condition E(mRᵉ) = 0, which delivers standard errors that are robust to non-normal or autocorrelated errors. You can cite this when someone worries that normality is needed.</p>\n<p data-b=\"55\"><strong>Cross-sectional regression</strong> (pp. 235 onward). Two passes: (1) estimate betas from time-series regressions, (2) regress average returns on betas to estimate λ: E(Rᵉᵢ) = βᵢ′λ + αᵢ. Used when the factor is not a traded return (for example a macro or climate-news variable). The pricing errors αᵢ should be jointly zero.</p>\n<p data-b=\"56\"><strong>Fama–MacBeth procedure</strong> (pp. 245–249).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"57\">Estimate betas (full sample or rolling window).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"58\">At <em>each date t</em> run a cross-sectional regression of returns on betas: Rᵉᵢₜ = βᵢ λₜ + αᵢₜ.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"59\">Average the λₜ over time to get λ̂, and use the time-series standard deviation of λ̂ₜ to get its standard error (divide the variance by T).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"60\">The logic of why it works (p. 246): the sampling variation of an estimate across samples can be learned from how it varies across subperiods.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"61\"><strong>Quotable (p. 246):</strong> \"Sampling error is, after all, about how a statistic would vary from one sample to the next if we repeated the observations.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"62\">Two cautions that matter for a thesis that uses panel data on banks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"63\"><strong>Cross-sectional correlation</strong> (pp. 247–248): if you pool firms and years and ignore that errors are correlated across firms in the same year, OLS standard errors are far too small. Cochrane: \"the standard errors so computed are often off by a factor of 10\" (p. 248). If the regressors do not change over time, Fama–MacBeth, a cross-sectional regression on averages, and pooled OLS with a correction are numerically the same (the proposition on p. 248).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"64\"><strong>Generated regressors</strong> (p. 249): Fama–MacBeth does not correct for the fact that betas are estimated. Use the Shanken correction and check it is not large. Also, with corporate-finance panels, errors are likely correlated over time as well as across firms, so use GMM-based errors that handle both.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"65\" id=\"b65\">1.7 What the evidence says (Chapter 20.3, pp. 449–453)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"66\">This is the part to use when you need a one-paragraph summary of two decades of empirical asset pricing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"67\">Prices carry information about expected returns: low price-to-dividend, price-to-earnings or book-to-market signals high future returns, because discount rates move more than cash-flow expectations.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"68\"><strong>Magnifying glasses</strong> (p. 449): small high-frequency predictability becomes large at long horizons for slow-moving predictors, and sorting into portfolios makes small effects economically big.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"69\"><strong>Dogs that did not bark</strong> (pp. 449–450): dividends do not forecast with the sign they would need if returns were unpredictable, so the variation must be in expected returns.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"70\">The patterns point to premia for recession and financial-distress risk that is <em>not</em> market risk (p. 449, p. 450).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"71\"><strong>Quotable (p. 451):</strong> \"We have to get used to the fact that most returns and price variation come from variation in risk premia, not variation in expected cash flows, interest rates, etc.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"72\"><strong>Quotable (p. 449):</strong> \"Prices reveal slow-moving market expectations of subsequent excess returns, because potential offsetting events seem sluggish or absent.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"73\"><strong>Doubts</strong> (pp. 452–453). Momentum lacks a macro-risk story; premia often shrink after being published or after funds exploit them (the small-firm effect \"completely disappeared in 1980\", p. 452). Cochrane’ s phrase for the instability: \"In the entire history of finance there has always been an anomaly-du-jour, and momentum is it right now\" (p. 452). The CAPM allows extra common movement, but not extra <em>average return</em> for it (p. 453).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"74\"><strong>Use in thesis:</strong> a climate or transition-risk premium has to pass the same three tests: (1) is it tied to bad states (so the sign is justified), (2) does it survive publication and out-of-sample, (3) are standard errors corrected for cross-sectional correlation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"75\"><strong>File flag.</strong> The PDF name says 2005; the text I used is the revised edition’ s printed pagination (553 pp.). Cite the printed pages above, not PDF page numbers. Chapter 20.1–20.2 and Chapter 21 detail were not read, so I have not summarised the long-horizon regressions or the consumption-based solutions to the puzzle.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"76\" id=\"b76\">2. MacKinlay (1997), Event Studies in Economics and Finance: reading the file and writing the notes</h1>\n<p data-b=\"77\"><strong>What was read.</strong> All 30 PDF pages, cover to references. The paper is A. Craig MacKinlay, \"Event Studies in Economics and Finance\", <em>Journal of Economic Literature</em> 35 (March 1997), pp. 13–39. <strong>File flag:</strong> your file name says 1996; the paper itself is dated 1997, so cite 1997. <strong>Scan flag:</strong> the PDF is an image with no text layer, so I OCR’ d it. Equations and the numbers in tables 1 and 2 may carry OCR slips, so any figure you quote should be checked against the PDF image. Page numbers below are the journal’ s <strong>printed</strong> pages (PDF page 1 = p. 13).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"78\" id=\"b78\">2.1 What an event study is for</h2>\n<p data-b=\"79\">An event study measures how a specific event changes the value of a firm, using security prices over a short period around the event.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"80\"><strong>Quotable (p. 13):</strong> \"given rationality in the marketplace, the effects of an event will be reflected immediately in security prices.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"81\">That one assumption (informationally efficient prices) is the whole justification. A short window of stock returns replaces months of waiting for accounting or productivity data. Applications named on pp. 13–14: mergers, earnings announcements, debt and equity issues, macro announcements, and <strong>changes in the regulatory environment</strong> (Schwert 1981). The modern method comes from Ball and Brown (1968) and Fama et al. (1969) (p. 14).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"82\" id=\"b82\">2.2 The seven-step procedure (Section 2, pp. 14–16)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"83\"><strong>Define the event and the event window.</strong> Make the window longer than the one day of interest, normally at least the announcement day and the next day, because news released after the close shows up the next day (p. 15). Pre-event days let you check for leakage.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"84\"><strong>Select the firms</strong> (data availability, industry) and describe the sample, noting any selection bias (p. 15).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"85\"><strong>Measure the abnormal return</strong>: actual return minus the normal return the firm would have had without the event.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"86\"><strong>Estimate the normal-return model on an estimation window</strong> that comes before the event window and does not overlap it, for example the 120 days before the event (p. 15).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"87\"><strong>Choose the test and the aggregation</strong> (null hypothesis, how to combine firms and days).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"88\"><strong>Present results and diagnostics.</strong> Check whether one or two firms drive everything (p. 15).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"89\"><strong>Interpret</strong>: separate competing explanations (p. 16).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"90\"><strong>Quotable (p. 15), the definition to cite:</strong> \"The abnormal return is the actual ex post return of the security over the event window minus the normal return of the firm over the event window.\" Formally, ARᵢₜ = Rᵢₜ − E(Rᵢₜ Xₜ) (eq. 1).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"91\" id=\"b91\">2.3 Normal-return models (Section 4, pp. 17–19)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"92\"><strong>Constant mean return</strong>: Rᵢₜ = μᵢ + ζᵢₜ (eq. 2). Simple, and Brown and Warner (1980, 1985) find it often gives results similar to fancier models (p. 17).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"93\"><strong>Market model</strong>: Rᵢₜ = αᵢ + βᵢ Rₘₜ + εᵢₜ (eq. 3, p. 18). It strips out the part of the return explained by the market, so the abnormal return has lower variance and the test is sharper. The gain depends on the regression R². Popular market proxies: S&amp;P 500, CRSP value- or equal-weighted index (p. 18).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"94\"><strong>Other statistical models</strong>: multifactor models (industry index, size-matched portfolio). Gains are limited because extra factors add little beyond the market factor, except when all the sample firms share a characteristic such as one industry, which is exactly the case for banks (p. 18).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"95\"><strong>Market-adjusted model</strong>: a restricted market model with α = 0 and β = 1, used only when no estimation window exists, for example IPOs (pp. 18–19). MacKinlay’ s advice: use restricted models only if necessary.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"96\"><strong>Economic models (CAPM, APT)</strong> (p. 19): use of the CAPM in event studies \"has almost ceased\" because known CAPM deviations make the restriction risky and the plain market model avoids that at little cost. APT-motivated models add little because the first factor behaves like the market.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"97\">The statistical models assume returns are jointly normal and i.i.d. (p. 17). MacKinlay says the assumption is strong but inference is robust to departures, and that a GMM approach makes the analysis autocorrelation- and heteroskedasticity-consistent.</p>\n<p data-b=\"98\"><strong>Connection to Cochrane (Section 1 of this doc):</strong> the market model is the one-factor beta model; a multifactor normal-return model is a factor model m = a + b′f. If you add a climate factor to the normal-return model, you are doing a Cochrane-style factor model inside an event study.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"99\" id=\"b99\">2.4 Mechanics: windows, estimation, aggregation (Section 5, pp. 19–24)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"100\"><strong>Notation (p. 19, Figure 1 on p. 20).</strong> Event date t = 0. Estimation window T₀+1 to T₁, length L₁. Event window T₁+1 to T₂, length L₂. Optional post-event window T₂+1 to T₃.</p>\n<p data-b=\"101\"><strong>Why windows do not overlap</strong> (p. 20): if the event returns enter the estimation, the normal-return parameters absorb part of the event effect and the abnormal return understates it.</p>\n<p data-b=\"102\"><strong>Estimation</strong> (eqs. 4–6, p. 20): OLS of the stock return on the market over the estimation window. OLS is consistent and efficient under the assumptions.</p>\n<p data-b=\"103\"><strong>Abnormal return</strong> (eq. 7): ARᵢₜ = Rᵢₜ − α̂ᵢ − β̂ᵢ Rₘₜ.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"104\"><strong>Quotable (p. 20):</strong> \"The abnormal return is the disturbance term of the market model calculated on an out of sample basis.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"105\"><strong>Variance</strong> (eq. 8, p. 21): σ²(ARᵢₜ) = σ²ε + a term for sampling error in α̂, β̂. With a long estimation window the second term vanishes, so the abnormal returns are about independent over time with variance σ²ε. Under H₀ (no effect on mean or variance), AR ~ N(0, σ²) (eq. 9).</p>\n<p data-b=\"106\"><strong>Cumulative abnormal return (CAR)</strong> (eqs. 10–12, p. 21): CARᵢ(τ₁,τ₂) = sum of ARᵢₜ from τ₁ to τ₂; its variance is (τ₂ − τ₁ + 1) σ²ε for a long estimation window.</p>\n<p data-b=\"107\"><strong>Across firms</strong> (eqs. 13–20, pp. 22–24): average AR across N firms, then cumulate over time (or cumulate by firm then average, which is equivalent, eq. 17). If event windows do not overlap, covariances are zero and the variance of the average is the average of variances divided by N. The test statistic is the CAR divided by its standard error, asymptotically standard normal (eq. 20, p. 24). Patell (1976) suggests standardising each abnormal return first.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"108\" id=\"b108\">2.5 MacKinlay’ s worked example (Sections 3 and 5.D, pp. 16–17, 24–27)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"109\"><strong>Design:</strong> 30 Dow Jones firms, 1989–1993, 600 quarterly earnings announcements, split by I/B/E/S surprise into good news (more than 2.5% above expectation), no news and bad news (189 / 173 / 238 events). Daily returns, 41-day event window (20 days before, day 0, 20 after), 250-day estimation window (p. 17).</p>\n<p data-b=\"110\"><strong>Results (pp. 24–26):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"111\">Day 0 average abnormal return: <strong>+0.965%</strong> for good news (standard error 0.104%, test statistic 9.28) and <strong>−0.679%</strong> for bad news (standard error 0.098%, statistic −6.93). No-news firms: −0.091%, under one standard error from zero.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"112\">Day 1 effect (+0.251% and −0.204%) is explained by announcements made after the close.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"113\">Constant-mean model gives the same conclusion but larger standard errors (0.130 / 0.124 / 0.131 against 0.104 / 0.098 / 0.098). This is the variance reduction from the market model in action.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"114\">The CAR plots show the market learns gradually: good-news firms drift up and bad-news firms drift down in days −20 to −1 (p. 26), i.e. partial anticipation.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"115\" id=\"b115\">2.6 Clustering (Section 5.E, pp. 27–28) <strong>← crucial for your bank sample</strong></h2>\n<p data-b=\"116\">If event windows overlap in calendar time, abnormal returns are cross-correlated and the variance formula in 2.4 is wrong (Bernard 1987). Two fixes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"117\"><strong>Portfolio approach</strong>: aggregate the firms into one portfolio in event time and run the single-series test. This allows for cross-correlation.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"118\"><strong>Multivariate regression with event-date dummies</strong> (Schipper and Thompson 1983, 1985; Collins and Dent 1984): needed when there is <strong>total clustering</strong>, the same event on the same day for many firms. It allows some firms to move up and others down, but the test often has poor finite-sample properties and low power (p. 28).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"119\" id=\"b119\">2.7 Relaxing the null and testing risk (Section 6, pp. 28–29)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"120\">If the event also raises variance, estimating variance from the pre-event window understates it. Fix: use the cross-section of CARs to estimate the variance, equation 21 (Boehmer, Musumeci and Poulsen 1991). It requires no cross-sectional correlation, which is again a no-clustering condition, but not homoskedasticity. To test for a change in risk, let the market-model beta shift over the event window (Kane and Unal 1988).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"121\" id=\"b121\">2.8 Power (Section 7, pp. 28–31)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"122\">Power is the probability of rejecting the null when the abnormal return is really non-zero. MacKinlay tabulates it for a 5% two-sided test (Table 2, p. 29), for abnormal returns of 0.5%, 1.0%, 1.5%, 2.0%, and for two values of the CAR standard deviation: <strong>2%</strong> (variance 0.0004; one-day window, no variance increase) and <strong>4%</strong> (variance 0.0016; multi-day window or event raises variance).</p>\n<p data-b=\"123\">Useful numbers from his discussion (pp. 30–31):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"124\">With a 2% standard deviation and a 0.5% abnormal return, power with 20 firms is only 0.20, and you need more than 60 firms to reach 0.50.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"125\">With a 2.0% abnormal return and 20 firms, power is 0.99.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"126\">With a 4% standard deviation, a 0.5% abnormal return gives power 0.09 with 20 firms and 0.42 with 200 firms; with a 1.5% abnormal return and 30 firms, 0.54.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"127\"><strong>Quotable (p. 31):</strong> if power is not sufficient, \"search for ways of increasing the power. This can be done by increasing the sample size, shortening the event window, or by developing more specific predictions to test.\" (The sentence runs from p. 31 to p. 32; quote it as one passage.)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"128\" id=\"b128\">2.9 Nonparametric tests (Section 8, p. 32)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"129\"><strong>Sign test</strong>: under H₀ the proportion of positive CARs is 0.5; statistic (eq. 23) = <span>[</span>N⁺/N − 0.5<span>]</span> √N / 0.5 ~ N(0,1). Weakness: misspecified when abnormal returns are skewed (daily data).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"130\"><strong>Corrado (1989) rank test</strong>: rank each firm’ s abnormal returns across the window; the event-day rank has expected value (L₂+1)/2 under H₀ (eq. 24). Robust to skewness.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"131\">Use them <em>alongside</em> the parametric test as a robustness check, not instead. Campbell and Wasley (1993) found the rank test more reliable than parametric tests for NASDAQ daily returns (p. 32).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"132\" id=\"b132\">2.10 Cross-sectional regressions of abnormal returns (Section 9, pp. 33–34)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"133\">Regress each event’ s AR (or CAR) on firm characteristics: ARⱼ = δ₀ + δ₁ x₁ⱼ + … + ηⱼ (eq. 26). Use <strong>White heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors</strong> because residuals will not be homoskedastic (p. 33). Example: Asquith and Mullins (1986) regressed the two-day CAR for equity-issue announcements on offering size and prior CAR.</p>\n<p data-b=\"134\"><strong>Pitfall (p. 33):</strong> characteristics can predict how much of the event was <em>anticipated</em>, which creates selection bias and inconsistent OLS. Solutions: model the selection explicitly (Acharya 1988; Eckbo et al. 1990); Prabhala (1995) argues OLS with weak conditions still gives t-statistics that are lower bounds on significance.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"135\" id=\"b135\">2.11 Other issues (Section 10, pp. 34–36)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"136\"><strong>Sampling interval</strong> (p. 34): shorter is much better for power. With 50 securities and a 1% abnormal return, power is 0.94 with daily data, 0.35 weekly, 0.12 monthly.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"137\"><strong>Event-date uncertainty</strong> (p. 35): widen the window to days 0 and +1; Ball and Torous (1988) show the informal fix works well.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"138\"><strong>Robustness</strong> (p. 35): normality matters only for exact finite-sample results; convergence is fast (Brown and Warner 1985).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"139\"><strong>Biases</strong> (pp. 35–36): nonsynchronous trading (adjust beta by Scholes–Williams; for liquid stocks the effect is small), and an upward bias in CARs for illiquid stocks with wide bid–ask spreads (Blume and Stambaugh 1983); fix by using buy-and-hold returns.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"140\" id=\"b140\">2.12 Conclusion and limits (Section 11, pp. 36–38)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"141\">Successes: mergers (targets earn large positive abnormal returns, over 20% in Jarrell and Poulsen’ s 663-takeover sample; acquirers about zero), and external financing (equity issues about −2.7% to −3.6% over two days; debt issues about −0.23%), which motivated the pecking-order theory. Courts have implicitly accepted the method (Basic v. Levinson, 1988) (p. 37).</p>\n<p data-b=\"142\"><strong>Where it works poorly, and why this matters to you:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"143\"><strong>Quotable (p. 37), regulatory events:</strong> \"the wealth effects of regulatory changes for affected entities can be difficult to detect using event study methodology. The problem is that regulatory changes are often debated in the political arena over time and any accompanying wealth effects generally will gradually be incorporated into the value of a corporation as the probability of the change being adopted increases.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"144\"><strong>Quotable (p. 38), closing line:</strong> \"event studies have shown that, as would be expected in a rational marketplace, prices do respond to new information.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"145\" id=\"b145\">2.13 Using this in your thesis</h2>\n<p data-b=\"146\">If your design studies how public-sector bank stock prices react to climate-policy or climate-regulation announcements (for example international climate agreements or RBI/SEBI disclosure and stress-testing circulars), MacKinlay tells you what to worry about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"147\"><strong>Total clustering.</strong> Every bank gets the same news on the same day, so the standard N-independent-firms test is invalid (pp. 27–28). Use the portfolio approach, or the dummy-variable multivariate regression, and say so explicitly.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"148\"><strong>Low power with a small sample.</strong> You will have about a dozen public-sector banks. From Table 2 (p. 29, read from OCR) with 12 firms: at a 2% CAR standard deviation, a 1% abnormal return is detected with power about 0.41; at a 4% standard deviation, about 0.14. Report power, use daily data and short windows, and do not read a non-rejection as no effect (pp. 30–32).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"149\"><strong>Anticipation and slow-moving policy.</strong> MacKinlay’ s own warning about regulatory events (p. 37) applies. Consider dating the event as the days when the <em>probability</em> of adoption changed (Schipper and Thompson approach, p. 38), and show pre-event CARs.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"150\"><strong>Normal return model.</strong> The market model is the default (p. 19). With banks, an industry/banking index as an additional factor is the case MacKinlay says is worth the multifactor model (p. 18).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"151\"><strong>Second stage.</strong> Regress CARs on bank characteristics, for example capital adequacy, brown exposure, NPA ratio, with White standard errors (p. 33), and keep the anticipation caveat in mind.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"152\"><strong>Robustness.</strong> Add the Corrado rank or sign test alongside the parametric test (p. 32).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"153\"><strong>What was not read:</strong> the cited papers (Brown and Warner 1980/1985, Corrado 1989, Schipper and Thompson, Boehmer et al., Prabhala). Details of those tests are as MacKinlay summarises them.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"154\" id=\"b154\">3. Survey-based folder: Stroebel and Wurgler (2021), the working paper survey and the supplement</h1>\n<p data-b=\"155\"><strong>What was read.</strong> All three files in full: the published paper (Stroebel and Wurgler, \"What do you think about climate finance?\", <em>Journal of Financial Economics</em> 142 (2021), pp. 487–498, 12 pp.), the NBER working-paper version (No. 29136, August 2021, 26 pp.) and the Internet Appendix (5 pp.). Page numbers below are the <strong>journal’ s printed pages (487–498)</strong>. <strong>Flags:</strong> the Internet Appendix figures A1 (the survey invitation) and A2 (example questions) are images and were not read; I read its tables A1 and A2 only. For the working paper I compared the text, the Table 3 numbers and the conclusion against the published version; I did not check every cell of every table.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"156\" id=\"b156\">3.1 What this paper is, and what it is not</h2>\n<p data-b=\"157\">It is an <strong>opinion survey</strong> of finance academics, finance professionals and public-sector economists about climate finance. It reports what experts <em>believe</em> about climate risks and asset prices, not evidence about what prices actually do. That distinction is the first thing to say when you cite it.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"158\"><strong>Quotable (abstract, p. 487):</strong> \"By an overwhelming margin, respondents believe that asset prices underestimate climate risks rather than overestimate them.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"159\">Why it belongs in the layer on asset pricing: it is the survey-based counterpart of Cochrane’ s question. Cochrane asks whether risks that covary with bad states are priced; Stroebel and Wurgler ask the experts whether climate risk is priced enough and whether climate risk covaries with good or bad times. It is also written by two of the people behind the Giglio–Kelly–Stroebel review in Layer 6.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"160\" id=\"b160\">3.2 Survey design (Section 2, pp. 488–489)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"161\"><strong>Population targeted:</strong> (i) professors at the top 100 finance departments, ranked by publications in the <em>Journal of Finance</em>, <em>JFE</em> and <em>RFS</em> in 2010–2020 (3,570 email addresses); (ii) NYU Stern graduates working in finance (6,921); (iii) researchers and policymakers at 17 public institutions, among them central banks, the IMF and the World Bank (955). Total 11,446 email addresses (p. 488).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"162\"><strong>Instrument:</strong> a Qualtrics online survey, one recruitment email per person, anonymous, 5–7 minutes, run in <strong>July 2021</strong>. Answer options were randomly ordered (table notes, pp. 493–497).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"163\"><strong>Response:</strong> 861 complete responses, a 7.5% response rate. Faculty 453 (12.7% of those contacted), practitioners 294 (4.2%), regulators/public sector 72 (7.5%); 42 did not give an occupation (p. 488). The authors compare this with 9% for the Graham–Harvey CFO survey and 4.3% for McCahery et al. (p. 488).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"164\"><strong>Who answered</strong> (Table 1, p. 490): 55% faculty, 36% private sector, 9% public sector; 72% North America, 14% Europe, 7% Asia, 7% rest of world; 69% say they personally are concerned about climate change; 59% work on climate finance; 45% received their highest degree in 2010 or later.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"165\"><strong>Sample-selection bias, admitted by the authors:</strong></p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"166\"><strong>Quotable (p. 488):</strong> \"Respondents are probably more interested in climate finance than nonrespondents.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"167\">They argue this is \"not entirely unhelpful\" because you may prefer the views of the most informed, and they check whether answers differ by climate concern and professional interest (p. 488–489). The Internet Appendix adds 158 respondents who found the survey on Twitter and LinkedIn and reports that results are broadly similar (footnote 6, p. 488).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"168\" id=\"b168\">3.3 Findings, with the numbers</h2>\n<p data-b=\"169\"><strong>A. Which climate risks matter most?</strong> (Section 3.1, p. 489; Table 2, p. 493). Respondents ranked five risks, 1 = most important:</p>\n<table data-b=\"170\">\n<caption><strong>Table 6.2.</strong> Survey-based folder: Stroebel and Wurgler (2021): 3.3 Findings, with the numbers</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Next 5 years (average rank)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Next 30 years (average rank)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Regulatory</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>1.9</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stakeholder (customers, employees)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Physical</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>2.2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Technological</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Legal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"171\">Regulatory (transition) risk is the top short-run risk, \"ranked a full position higher, on average, than the second-most-cited risk\" (p. 489). Physical risk is the top 30-year risk. Public-sector respondents already rated physical risk second over five years (p. 489). The exception to physical-first at 30 years is the low-concern group.</p>\n<p data-b=\"172\"><strong>B. Are asset prices right?</strong> (Section 3.2, p. 489–491; Table 3, p. 494). Pooled shares:</p>\n<table data-b=\"173\">\n<caption><strong>Table 6.3.</strong> Survey-based folder: Stroebel and Wurgler (2021): 3.3 Findings, with the numbers (b)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Market</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Too much</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Correct</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Not enough</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">No opinion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stock markets</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">21%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>60%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">16%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Real estate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">17%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>67%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Insurance</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">25%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>42%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">30%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"174\">\"Not enough\" outnumbers \"too much\" 20 to 1 for stocks (60:3), 67 to 1 for real estate and 21 to 1 for insurance (42:2) (p. 489). It holds in every subgroup, even among the low-concern respondents. Differences: private-sector respondents (73%) are 22 points more likely than academics (51%) to say stocks underprice climate risk, \"perhaps a result of a stronger belief among finance academics in the efficiency of markets\" (p. 491). Those who think prices are too low rank <strong>physical risk</strong> higher (p. 491).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"175\"><strong>Quotable (p. 491):</strong> \"Either the widespread belief that asset prices and insurance markets insufficiently price climate risk is way off, or these markets have a lot catching up to do.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"176\"><strong>C. Do climate payoffs come in good or bad times, and what discount rate?</strong> (Section 3.3, pp. 491–492; Table 4, p. 495).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"177\">A mitigation project pays off: good times <strong>32%</strong>, bad times <strong>13%</strong>, equally in both <strong>55%</strong>.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"178\">Median social discount rate for a project with certain benefits 50 years out: <strong>4%</strong>; with uncertain benefits: <strong>7%</strong>; so the median <strong>risk premium is 3%</strong> (public sector 1%, private sector 4%).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"179\">The authors frame the theory (p. 491). Climate change is modelled either as a tax on consumption that grows with output (Nordhaus 2008) or as a disaster that triggers a downturn (Barro 2013; Weitzman 2012, 2014).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"180\"><strong>Quotable (p. 491), the pricing logic:</strong> \"If climate change is more costly in good economic times, then investments to mitigate climate change pay off disproportionately in those times and deserve a positive risk premium. On the other hand, if investments to mitigate climate change pay of largely in bad economic times, they should be considered hedges that command a negative risk premium.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"181\">This is Cochrane’ s p = E(x)/Rᶠ + cov(m, x) in words: a payoff that comes in good times (low m) is worth less than its expected value, so it must be discounted at a higher rate. Respondents who say \"good times\" are three times more numerous than \"bad times\", so the median respondent applies a positive premium (pp. 491–492). The authors note the question forced a single static rate (footnote 11, p. 491).</p>\n<p data-b=\"182\"><strong>D. What will force firms to change?</strong> (Section 3.4, p. 492; Table 5, p. 496). Share choosing each in their top three: carbon taxes <strong>52%</strong>, institutional investors <strong>48%</strong>, government subsidies 43%, customers 41%, non-financial regulation 27%, financial regulation 22%, banks and creditors 16%, employees 6%, individual investors 5%, voluntary corporate action <strong>0%</strong> (p. 492: \"not one respondent was optimistic that voluntary behavior by corporations … would be a significant force\"). Private-sector respondents prefer institutional investors and customers; faculty and public sector prefer carbon taxes.</p>\n<p data-b=\"183\"><strong>E. What should research work on?</strong> (Section 3.5, pp. 492 and 498; Table 6, p. 497). Top three: effects of government incentives (35%), pricing of climate risk in financial markets (34%), <strong>climate change effect on systemic risk (28%; 47% among public-sector respondents)</strong>, real effects of socially responsible investing (23%), new financial instruments (21%). Stress tests: 13%. The authors compare these priorities with 420 climate-finance papers uploaded to SSRN-FEN over three years: the rank correlation is 0.85, 0.91 for those who work on climate finance and 0.64 for outsiders (p. 498). But some topics are under-researched relative to stated importance, among them systemic risk (28% of respondents vs 15% of SSRN papers) and stress tests (13% vs 4%) (Table 6, p. 497; text p. 498).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"184\" id=\"b184\">3.4 Working paper vs published version</h2>\n<p data-b=\"185\">The NBER working paper (No. 29136, August 2021) and the JFE article give the same survey, the same sample and the same results. The differences I found are minor: the working paper lists extra JEL codes (G32, Q56); its introduction says \"There was a strong response to our survey request\" where the journal text opens with the uniformity of opinion; a few reference years differ (for example Bansal, Kiku and Ochoa 2017 vs 2018 in the body text); and the working paper has \"<span>[</span>INSERT TABLE n NEAR HERE<span>]</span>\" markers with the tables at the end. Cite the <strong>published</strong> article.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"186\" id=\"b186\">3.5 What the supplement adds (Internet Appendix, tables A1 and A2)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"187\"><strong>Extended sample:</strong> adds the Twitter/LinkedIn respondents and splits climate concern into four levels: very high (33%), high (37%), low (26%), no concern (4%).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"188\"><strong>The \"no concern\" group answers very differently</strong> (Table A2). Over five years they rank physical risk 4.6 (vs 3.1 pooled); only 8% say stocks underprice climate risk and 22% say \"too much\"; their median discount rate for risk-free benefits is 7% and for uncertain benefits 17% (a 10-point premium). This is a small group (about 4% of the sample), but it shows the \"consensus\" is concentrated among people who think climate change is real and serious.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"189\">High-concern respondents who work in climate finance are the most convinced prices are too low (82% for stocks).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"190\" id=\"b190\">3.6 Using this in your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"191\"><strong>Support for the transition-risk framing.</strong> Experts rank regulatory (transition) risk first over five years and physical risk first over 30 years (Table 2, p. 493). That matches a thesis horizon of capital adequacy over the next few years in banks exposed to transition risk. Use it as expert-opinion support, not as evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"192\"><strong>Market underpricing as motivation.</strong> Cite the 60:3 and 20-to-1 numbers (p. 489) as evidence that experts expect climate risk is not fully priced, which is the reason a bank-capital or event-study analysis might find reactions to climate news. Pair it with Cochrane on why a premium need not exist for idiosyncratic risk.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"193\"><strong>Gap in the literature.</strong> Systemic risk and stress tests are rated far more important than the volume of research on them (Table 6, p. 497). For a thesis on capital adequacy this is a ready-made sentence in the introduction. Public-sector respondents rate systemic risk at 47%.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"194\"><strong>Discount rate for climate risk.</strong> The 4% / 7% / 3% medians (Table 4, p. 495) are a reference point if you discuss how to value long-dated climate losses on bank balance sheets.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"195\"><strong>Limits to state:</strong> 72% of respondents are from North America and only about 7% (roughly 60 people) from Asia; none are India-specific in the tabulations I read; practitioners are largely NYU Stern alumni; response rate 7.5%; it was run in July 2021; it measures belief, not outcomes (p. 488). If you quote it for the Indian context, say so.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"196\"><strong>Quotable (conclusion, p. 498):</strong> \"Scientists often describe climate change with superlatives. Urgent. Dire. Existential. The superlatives are all bad.\" and \"As with other aspects of climate change, time will tell whether these beliefs are justified.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1 data-b=\"197\" id=\"b197\">4. Master quotables list and how Layer 5 feeds the thesis</h1>\n<h2 data-b=\"198\" id=\"b198\">4.1 The quotables, ranked by usefulness</h2>\n<p data-b=\"199\">Page numbers are printed pages of each source. Quote exactly as shown and add the page when you cite.</p>\n<table data-b=\"200\">\n<caption><strong>Table 6.4.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 5 feeds the thesis: 4.1 The quotables, ranked by usefulness</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source and page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Use it to</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cochrane, p. 7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"All asset pricing models amount to alternative ways of connecting the stochastic discount factor to data.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Say that CAPM, ICAPM, APT and a climate factor are all versions of one idea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cochrane, p. 151</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"all factor models are derived as specializations of the consumption-based model.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justify why a factor model (including a climate factor) has a theoretical basis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cochrane, p. 451</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"We have to get used to the fact that most returns and price variation come from variation in risk premia, not variation in expected cash flows, interest rates, etc.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Argue that climate repricing can show up as a change in risk premia, not in expected cash flows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cochrane, p. 449</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Prices reveal slow-moving market expectations of subsequent excess returns, because potential offsetting events seem sluggish or absent.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Summarise the empirical asset-pricing evidence in one line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cochrane, p. 248</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the standard errors so computed are often off by a factor of 10\" (about pooled regressions that ignore cross-sectional correlation)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justify clustered or Fama–MacBeth standard errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">MacKinlay, p. 13</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"given rationality in the marketplace, the effects of an event will be reflected immediately in security prices.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">State the assumption behind any event study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">MacKinlay, p. 15</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The abnormal return is the actual ex post return of the security over the event window minus the normal return of the firm over the event window.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Define abnormal return in your methodology chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">MacKinlay, p. 37</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the wealth effects of regulatory changes for affected entities can be difficult to detect using event study methodology.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acknowledge the limit of event studies for policy events, and justify a design that dates probability changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stroebel and Wurgler, p. 487</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"By an overwhelming margin, respondents believe that asset prices underestimate climate risks rather than overestimate them.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Motivate the question of whether climate risk is priced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stroebel and Wurgler, p. 488</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Respondents are probably more interested in climate finance than nonrespondents.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">State the survey’ s selection bias when you cite it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">11</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stroebel and Wurgler, p. 491</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"If climate change is more costly in good economic times, then investments to mitigate climate change pay off disproportionately in those times and deserve a positive risk premium.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Link climate risk to the sign of a risk premium (Cochrane’ s covariance logic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stroebel and Wurgler, p. 491</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Either the widespread belief that asset prices and insurance markets insufficiently price climate risk is way off, or these markets have a lot catching up to do.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Frame the underpricing hypothesis fairly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"201\"><strong>Numbers worth keeping.</strong> Experts’ average ranks of regulatory risk over 5 years, 1.9 (Table 2, p. 493); stocks underpriced 60% vs overpriced 3% (Table 3, p. 494); median discount rate 4% risk-free, 7% for climate mitigation, premium 3% (Table 4, p. 495); systemic risk chosen by 28% of respondents (47% of the public sector) but 15% of SSRN papers (Table 6, p. 497); event-study power with 12 firms and a 1% abnormal return: about 0.41 at a 2% standard deviation and 0.14 at 4% (MacKinlay Table 2, p. 29, from OCR).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"202\" id=\"b202\">4.2 How Layer 5 feeds the thesis</h2>\n<p data-b=\"203\"><strong>The chain of logic.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"204\"><em>Cochrane</em> gives the theory: a risk that is correlated with bad states earns a premium; a diversifiable one does not. A climate factor is credible only if it moves marginal utility (p. 172) and survives tests with correct standard errors (pp. 245–249).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"205\"><em>MacKinlay</em> gives the empirical tool to see whether and when markets repriced a climate-related event, and the checklist of what can go wrong: clustering, anticipation, low power with few banks.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"206\"><em>Stroebel and Wurgler</em> give the expert-belief evidence that motivates both: markets are thought to underprice climate risk, regulation is the near-term risk, and systemic risk is under-researched.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"207\"><strong>Link to Layer 4 (financial contagion and systemic risk).</strong> Layer 4 explains how a shock to some banks spreads through balance sheets (Eisenberg–Noe, Acemoglu et al.) and how liquidity spirals amplify it (Brunnermeier–Pedersen). Layer 5 explains how markets <em>price</em> such a shock and how you would detect the repricing. Stroebel and Wurgler’ s Table 6 says systemic risk from climate is wanted by researchers and regulators but under-supplied.</p>\n<p data-b=\"208\"><strong>Link to Layer 6 (climate finance and central-bank view).</strong> Layer 6 covers the Green Swan, Carney’ s tragedy-of-the-horizon speech, the Giglio–Kelly–Stroebel review and applied stress-testing work. Read it with this layer in hand: when those papers talk about climate risk being \"priced\", \"underpriced\" or a \"factor\", they are using the Cochrane vocabulary in Section 1; when they use event-based evidence, they are using MacKinlay’ s. The Stroebel–Wurgler survey is by the same research group as the review, so it also tells you what that group thinks is still open.</p>\n<p data-b=\"209\"><strong>Where to use each in the thesis.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"210\">Literature review: Cochrane p. 7 and p. 151 (theory), Stroebel and Wurgler p. 487 (belief evidence).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"211\">Methodology chapter: MacKinlay pp. 15–24 (definitions and tests), pp. 27–28 (clustering), pp. 28–31 (power); Cochrane pp. 245–249 (Fama–MacBeth) if you run cross-sectional regressions.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"212\">Limitations chapter: MacKinlay p. 37 (regulatory events), Stroebel and Wurgler p. 488 (selection), Cochrane pp. 452–453 (premia can vanish).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"213\" id=\"b213\">4.3 What was and was not read</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"214\"><strong>Cochrane:</strong> read in full Chapters 1 (pp. 3–24) and 9 (pp. 149–174), Chapter 12 (pp. 229–249, up to the Fama–MacBeth discussion) and Chapter 20.3 (pp. 449–453). Not read: the remaining ~460 pages, including GMM theory, options, bonds, long-horizon regressions (20.1–20.2) and the consumption-model chapters (21 onwards).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"215\"><strong>MacKinlay:</strong> read in full from OCR. Check equation numbers and table values in the PDF before quoting them.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"216\"><strong>Stroebel and Wurgler:</strong> published paper, working paper and Internet Appendix tables read; Internet Appendix figures A1–A2 (images) not read.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"217\">The works these papers cite were not read.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"218\" id=\"b218\">4.4 File flags</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"219\">The MacKinlay file name says 1996; the paper is <em>Journal of Economic Literature</em> 35 (March 1997), pp. 13–39. Cite 1997.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"220\">The MacKinlay PDF is a scan without a text layer, which is why some table values come from OCR.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"221\">Cochrane is the revised edition’ s printed pagination; use these printed page numbers, not PDF page numbers.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"222\">The survey paper has an NBER working-paper twin; cite the <em>JFE</em> article (2021, 142, 487–498).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"223\"><strong>Next:</strong> Layer 6 builds directly on this one.</p>\n","s":[[126,"b1"],[127,"b20"],[127,"b22"],[128,"b29"],[128,"b35"],[129,"b40"],[129,"b42"],[129,"b51"],[130,"b65"],[131,"b76"],[131,"b78"],[132,"b82"],[132,"b91"],[133,"b99"],[133,"b108"],[134,"b115"],[134,"b119"],[134,"b121"],[134,"b128"],[135,"b132"],[135,"b135"],[135,"b140"],[136,"b145"],[136,"b154"],[136,"b156"],[137,"b160"],[137,"b168"],[139,"b184"],[139,"b186"],[139,"b190"],[140,"b197"],[140,"b198"],[141,"b202"],[142,"b213"],[143,"b218"]]};